Logseq desktop, built from upstream `master` (the database / 2.x line).

This recipe is not a self-contained build. It follows the upstream release
workflow `.github/workflows/build-desktop-release.yml`, which is the only
supported way to produce a desktop bundle at the moment:

  1. `pnpm install` + `gulp build`      -> resources/CSS synced into `static/`
  2. `pnpm cljs:release-electron`       -> shadow-cljs frontend + electron main
  3. `pnpm db-worker-node:bundle`       -> `dist/db-worker-node.js`
  4. `pnpm cli:release`                 -> OCaml/Melange CLI bundled to
                                           `static/logseq-cli.js`
  5. `pnpm webpack-app-build`           -> webpack app bundles
  6. `pnpm desktop:prepare-runtime-js`  -> runtime files staged under `static/js`
  7. electron-builder                   -> unpacked Electron tree

Heavy build dependencies, and why they are unavoidable:

  * `pnpm` + `nodejs` (>= 22.20) drive every JavaScript bundle.
  * `clojure` + a Java runtime compile the ClojureScript frontend and the
    Electron main process. The Maven and git dependencies are fetched on first
    build. Java is requested as the `java-runtime` virtual, so any installed
    JDK or JRE satisfies it; upstream CI also uses a JDK (`setup-java`, Java
    21), and `clojure` itself requires `java-environment`. Naming a concrete
    package here is not merely narrower: `jre-openjdk` conflicts with
    `jdk-openjdk`, so it demanded the removal of the JDK the build needs
    (2026-09-18).
  * `ocaml` + `opam` build the CLI runtime. Upstream removed the ClojureScript
    CLI and bundles `cli/` with Melange (`cli/dune`) before Vite packages it.
    The recipe creates its own switch under `$srcdir/opam-root`, so the
    builder's `~/.opam` is left alone, and pins OCaml 5.1.1 to match the
    release workflow.
  * `ccache` (and `mold`, when installed) are used for the two native Node
    addons that upstream compiles from source (`@zvec/zvec`, `keytar`). This is
    the only host-optimized code in the build; everything else is JavaScript.

Notes:

  * Electron is bundled from the version pinned in `resources/package.json`
    rather than taken from the repositories. The app ships its own
    `chrome-sandbox` helper (installed setuid root) and is packaged as an
    unpacked tree under `/opt/logseq-desktop-git`.
  * `/usr/bin/logseq` is a small launcher that reads extra flags from
    `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/logseq-flags.conf`.
  * The application installs its own CLI launcher into `~/.local/bin/logseq`
    on first run (upstream behaviour); that path normally precedes
    `/usr/bin`, so the CLI shadows the GUI launcher in an interactive shell.
  * Auto-update is a per-user setting (Settings -> General, stored in
    `~/.config/Logseq/configs.edn`). It is left at the upstream default.
  * Builds are long and network heavy. Expect several GB of downloaded
    dependencies and a full Electron tree in the package.

Command line:

  $ sudo fish build-all.fish --no-deps --install logseq-desktop-git

Or by hand, from this directory:

  $ makepkg -sr
  $ pacman -U logseq-desktop-git-*.pkg.tar.zst

Always regenerate metadata after touching the recipe:

  $ makepkg --printsrcinfo > .SRCINFO
